# Line endings, decided here rather than by each machine's git configuration.
#
# The tooling of this repository is bash first: the seeded profile, the CLI
# end-to-end suite, the packaging scripts and the launchers are shell, and the
# checksum files they read are matched line by line with an end-of-line anchor.
# A clone made on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which is the installer's
# default, rewrites every one of those files with CRLF. Bash then reads
# `set -euo pipefail\r` and answers "invalid option name", and a checksum line
# stops matching a pattern anchored on the end of the line: the archive is
# refused as unpinned while its hash sits right there in the file. Both were
# measured on 2026-09-07, on a Windows clone.
#
# So: every text file lands as LF in the working tree, on every system. Git
# for Windows handles LF files without complaint, and the editors this project
# uses are configured through .editorconfig.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions, and they are real: a Windows batch file is read by
# cmd.exe, which is the one interpreter here that wants CRLF, and a PowerShell
# script is read by the same family.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries git must never touch. Declared rather than left to detection: a
# heuristic that guesses wrong on a model file or an icon corrupts it silently.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.gguf binary
*.bin  binary
*.db   binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
